\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Loewner comparison over an ordered field}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{F} be an ordered field: its total order is compatible with
addition and multiplication of nonnegative elements. Let #{V} be a
finite-dimensional #{F}-vector space with a specified symmetric
\vocabk{bilinear form}{ca-0003} #{h} satisfying
#{h(v,v)>0} for every #{v\ne0}. An endomorphism #{A:V\to V} is
\vocabk{self-adjoint}{fdip-001P} for #{h} when
##{h(Av,w)=h(v,Aw)\qquad(v,w\in V).}
This pairing equation specifies the condition directly;
\ref{fdip-00K2} establishes the perfect duality that represents
adjoints in this setting. Write #{q_A(v)=h(v,Av)}.}

\p{A self-adjoint #{A} is a \newvocab{nonnegative operator}, written
#{A\geq0}, when #{q_A(v)\geq0} for every #{v}.
It is a \newvocab{positive-definite operator}, written #{A>0},
when #{q_A(v)>0} for every nonzero #{v}.
For self-adjoint #{A,B}, the \newvocab{Loewner order} is the comparison
##{A\leq B\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
       h(v,(B-A)v)\geq0\quad\text{for every }v\in V.}
The partial-order axioms, in the sense of [ordered sets](tt-002C),
are proved in \ref{fdip-00K3}.}

\p{This is the operator form of symmetry and diagonal nonnegativity
in Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.IsPosSemidef}, applied to
#{(v,w)\mapsto h(v,Aw)} with the identity scalar homomorphism.
That definition supplies the form-level condition; the specified
pairing identifies the form with the operator.}
